WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.030
After having been...

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:07.100
We've traveled as a family for
the past few Thanksgivings.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.400
And so this was sort of the first like...

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.960
Well, we did like the Friendsgiving
thing years ago, but--

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.710
Right.
This was kind of

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.020
a new group of people

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:24.486
and the people who invited
us we were very close to,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.990
but then they kind of
invited a lot of people

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.160
that they know that they're close to.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.770
So there was people that
I had seen like once

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.450
and there was people I'd never seen.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.070
There were like 25 people
at this Friendsgiving.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.670
Which is big in LA.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:40.810
Yeah, 'cause you got all these people

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.730
who don't go back home

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.740
and nobody is with their
extended family and so--

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.760
You don't wanna find
yourself alone at a Sheetz.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.910
And you got a lot of single people,

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.570
you got a lot of young
couples without children.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.930
And so I think it was
basically just me and my kids

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:00.840
and then one of their family

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.380
and their kids were the only
like families with kids.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.350
And then everybody else
was younger, more vibrant.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.550
I saw a picture of the group,

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.270
sharp looking group, big group.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.267
I'd say it was 24 people there.

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:14.220
It was 20 to 25.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.053
25 people.

00:01:15.053 --> 00:01:17.770
Yeah, yeah, and you dressed
up a little bit for it.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.340
I did?
I thought that you did.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.860
It looked like you were wearing a sweater.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:21.760
I had a sweater on, yeah.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.010
I have a Thanksgiving sweater on.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:23.920
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.059
It's made out of turkey feathers.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.079
If you'd been close enough
you could have seen that.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:31.960
I had to zoom in.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.308
I had on my cranberry pants.

00:01:33.308 --> 00:01:35.010
But you couldn't see that

00:01:36.508 --> 00:01:37.610
because of the angle.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.197
The pants were made out of like the--

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:40.950
The skins of cranberries.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.688
The skin hangs off at turkey's neck.

00:01:42.688 --> 00:01:43.521
Yeah, right.

00:01:43.521 --> 00:01:44.463
They're just bright red.

00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:48.383
And wrinkly. Looks like
leather, unless you get close.

00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:51.810
Like a turkey's nut sack.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.980
Scrotum pants.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.270
But the scrotum does hang
from the chin of a turkey.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.103
Dot.com.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.400
So I was very excited because,
you know me, I love to eat.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.990
And I was very excited because
the friend of the person

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.500
who was kind of throwing this was like,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.300
they're gonna take care of the food.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.800
A few people did make things to bring

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.800
like my wife made a sweet
potato casserole, Southern style

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.023
with some pecans on top, brown sugar,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.890
just traditional
Thanksgiving side, very good.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.070
And here's what I'll say.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.310
I call that a pre-dessert,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.170
not a side note dessert, but anyway.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.063
I enjoyed the meal quite a bit.

00:02:41.338 --> 00:02:44.463
You had your staples and you turkey gravy,

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.603
your cranberry sauce, chutney, whatever.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.000
And then like the sides
that people had brought.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.810
But I did notice something.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.320
First of all, there was
obviously a vegan option

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.630
for basically everything
and this is Los Angeles.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.520
So you got like a vegan option for the...

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.740
But it wasn't like a tofu turkey.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.103
They had like seitan,

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:08.950
which I call Satan.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.070
I just go straight for that.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.790
It's very big of you to invite Satan

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.410
to your Thanksgiving celebration.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.300
Satan steak, we have cut him up

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.809
and served him on Thanksgiving.

00:03:17.809 --> 00:03:19.709
Be thankful for his death.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.030
But the thing that I kinda noticed is that

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.760
the only thing on my plate
that had the quantities

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.560
of butter and sugar and flour
that I feel like constitutes

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.690
Thanksgiving was what my wife brought.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.830
And I gotta say, I know that
that's just a Southern boy

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.320
coming to Los Angeles.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.610
I specifically remember
one of the first potlucks

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:54.238
we ever came to when we
came out here, this--

00:03:54.238 --> 00:03:56.140
It was quite the eye-opening,
mouth-shutting experience.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.200
Yeah, yeah, it was like,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.740
all these people brought all these sides.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.390
And this one girl was like, "I made this.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:03.840
This is Paula Deen's recipe."

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.440
And I'm eating it, I'm like, "Really?"

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.717
And then as I'm eating it, she's like,

00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:08.810
"And you know what?

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.990
I used half the butter and half the sugar

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.850
that Paula called for
and it's still good."

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.703
And I'm thinking, "Paula knows best, bro.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.560
If you've seen the
silhouette of that woman,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.810
she knows how much butter
and sugar you're supposed

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.010
to put it things."

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:24.843
Right, right.

00:04:24.843 --> 00:04:27.210
And there's just a different mentality.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.043
And you know what?

00:04:28.043 --> 00:04:29.520
It's probably a better mentality.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.080
It's probably a mentality

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.970
that leads to a longer, healthier life.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:35.980
But when you--

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.740
Thanksgiving only comes once a year.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.853
But when you've decided to
throw caution to the wind,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.990
I could have--
Unfurl the sales.

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.210
I could have butter injected directly

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.860
into my veins on Thanksgiving.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.560
I would do that if that was a thing.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.760
We can make it a tradition.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.587
And so 'cause dessert time
happened and I was like,

00:04:56.587 --> 00:04:58.180
"Oh, there's a pumpkin pie."

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.150
And they had labeled everything

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.630
and it was pumpkin pie vegan.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:03.463
Vegan.

00:05:03.463 --> 00:05:04.657
And I was like .

00:05:05.974 --> 00:05:09.383
And there was like
chocolate pie raw vegan.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.153
What?

00:05:11.153 --> 00:05:14.663
And then it was like
some kind of bites vegan.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.453
Man
And--

00:05:17.453 --> 00:05:20.827
Now you know how vegan
people normally feel, right?

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:22.977
And so what I thought
was, I was just like,

00:05:22.977 --> 00:05:25.990
"Man, I just wanna go to Ralph's right now

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.390
and just get a pumpkin pie from Ralph's."

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.130
Just get a grocery store pumpkin pie,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.100
put some whipped cream on top
and then unbutton my pants

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:34.933
and enjoy myself.

00:05:34.933 --> 00:05:35.766
Right.

00:05:35.766 --> 00:05:36.599
You know?

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.070
So that was an adjustment.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:39.903
It was an adjustment

00:05:39.903 --> 00:05:42.150
that I feel like I can
be ready for next year.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.340
I think I'm gonna bring my
own butter and my own sugar.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.785
I'm just gonna squeeze, squeeze, squeeze.

00:05:49.785 --> 00:05:52.020
IV, bring an Ivy bag.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.140
Like squeegee a butter,

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.410
and then looks like a sugar shaker.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.302
And I'm just like, "Don't mind me."

00:05:58.134 --> 00:06:00.064
Just to kind of get
things up to the standard.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.220
Just bring stuff and label
it unapologetically unvegan.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.250
Yeah, right.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.631
That sounds like a good brand.

00:06:07.631 --> 00:06:08.831
Oh, yeah.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.008
And then the tagline is,
"Make it a tradition."

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.320
We sell it at Walmart and make millions.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.570
Don't commercialize my holiday slogan.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.950
I can't help but commercialize things.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.130
I helped a lot of people, man.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.320
But one--

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.180
I didn't make a dime off of it.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.260
One traditional thing that
happened is the host said,

00:06:30.127 --> 00:06:31.710
"We're gonna go around
and we're going to say

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.630
what we're thankful for
and we're gonna do..."

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.167
Because it's Los Angeles,
you kinda had to say,

00:06:35.167 --> 00:06:37.083
"We're gonna do the cliche thing.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.770
And we're gonna say what
we're thankful for."

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.540
And everybody was saying kind
of the traditional things,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.860
family and friends--

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.180
What was your gut, knee-jerk reaction

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.593
to that announcement?

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.310
Well, I--

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.310
I can think of three.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.150
What was my gut, knee-jerk
reaction to having--

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:03.690
To the announcement of the assignment

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.630
that this cliche thing was gonna happen?

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.480
Well, there's always a slight
increase in heart rate.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:08.313
Yeah?

00:07:08.313 --> 00:07:11.270
I think that every normal
person, even somebody who--

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.270
Don't speak for people.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:13.750
I'm asking for you.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.300
Don't normalize your response.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.240
Just tell me what yours was.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.790
I'm curious.
Well, a slight increase

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.940
in heart rate.
Okay.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.730
And then--
That's normal.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.270
Most people that would--

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.360
And then like, okay, I gotta say something

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.100
that sort of mixes humor
and heart in the right way.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.110
This is a branding moment.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.620
I don't exactly remember what
I said I was thankful for

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:38.900
'cause there was a second question

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.180
that I do remember everything
that I said which--

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:42.500
You don't remember what
you were thankful for?

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:45.670
No, it was something about
new friends and family

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.373
and then I made a joke.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:50.983
It went over well, trust me.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.160
But then the second thing that happened

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.500
while we were eating, which
I found very interesting

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.260
was there was a guy there
that was a life coach.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.700
Again, of course.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.050
Was he paid to be there?

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.050
No, if you get 25 people
together in Los Angeles,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.637
one of them will be a life coach.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:10.810
So--

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:11.753
That's code for something, right?

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:13.783
I dunno where--

00:08:13.783 --> 00:08:15.940
If you don't have one
life coach per 25 people

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.360
in Los Angeles, the entire city implodes.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.513
It's funny that the cashier
at Sheetz was a life coach.

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:26.743
So.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:28.770
Did he have like a tag?

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:30.620
How did you know he was a life coach?

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:32.360
Well--
A hat?

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.340
If someone is a life coach
or a vegan or into CrossFit--

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.030
You know it, yeah right.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:38.947
You know what I'm saying.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:41.417
You find out pretty early
in the conversation.

00:08:41.417 --> 00:08:43.520
Right, right.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.073
So I--
That's true.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.230
He stands up--
I got list of those things.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:51.823
He stands up and he says,

00:08:54.117 --> 00:08:55.610
"We're gonna all answer this question."

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:56.780
And again, I liked it

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.920
because it was intentional conversation

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.093
and it was 25 people at a
table and you're kinda talking

00:09:00.093 --> 00:09:01.570
to the people around you,

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.790
but then he's kind of putting
everybody on the spot.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.510
Even the kids, both of
my kids had to stand up

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:07.690
and answer this question.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.100
But it was the traditional question of,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.270
if you had a superpower, what would it be?

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.220
And what would be your first mission?

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.800
Oh, I actually haven't heard,

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:16.680
what will be your first mission?

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.930
First mission was new to me

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:18.763
'cause that's the kind of thing

00:09:18.763 --> 00:09:20.200
that a life coach adds to it.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.230
You understand?
Right, right.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:22.900
'Cause what's your favorite superpower?

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.070
That's just normal folk.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.360
What's your first mission?

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:27.340
Life coach.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.540
Business card, here you go.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.290
But the heart rate goes up
even more when that happens.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.633
Yeah, I'm like now--
Curve ball.

00:09:35.633 --> 00:09:38.124
Now, it's precious time to shine.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.490
So I said, and it was...

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:47.100
One guy requested, he said,
"Instead of going around,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.620
can we just do popcorn style?

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.150
Can we just, as you know,
what can use share?"

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:53.360
I was like, that's a good idea.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:54.660
Why is that a good idea?

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.170
He wanted to get dibs

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.860
on visibility or something.
Because of the inevitability

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.930
of it coming to you and
you're just thinking

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.830
about what you're gonna say.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.080
He had a reason, it was good.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:09.863
I thought it was great at the moment.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:14.610
But I had actually had
this exact discussion

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.470
with my wife the week before.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.590
Now, we've talked about what
our favorite superpowers

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.240
would be before we argued about it

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:22.550
at some point on a debate around my sink.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.480
If you believe in freedom,
you believe in flight.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.180
Invisibility is to hide
yourself from freedom.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.830
Like a shameful, naked mole rat.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.170
Don't be deceived, flight is not heroic.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:38.020
It is self-absorbed showboating.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.750
Look at me, I'm air swimming.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.480
I can't remember anything
that we do around here,

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.430
but I know we've talked about
superpowers and I personally--

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.330
Invisibility versus invincibility
was one of our first--

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:51.830
But we did invisibility
versus flight and then--

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.090
Yeah, we did.
I've always said

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.810
that teleportation is the
superpower that I would choose

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:01.800
because it technically can
replicate invisibility and flight

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.410
for all practical purposes.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:04.810
I would...

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:05.643
Are you asking me now?

00:11:05.643 --> 00:11:10.643
'Cause I would choose
being able to double myself

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:14.120
and be invisible so I
could creep on myself

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.830
and not even know it.

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:18.860
Well, interesting and disturbing.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.540
But I did not say that

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:22.820
because that's just a canned answer.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:23.653
I actually said what I
talked to my wife about.

00:11:23.653 --> 00:11:26.200
But this is Thanksgiving,
you don't eat canned ham.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:30.500
Jessie and I were with each
other, eating something,

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.700
and I was eating some bad food.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:33.610
Is this a dream?

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:34.443
No, no, no.
You lost me.

00:11:34.443 --> 00:11:36.170
This is the week before
that gave me the answer

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.287
to the superpower thing.
Okay.

00:11:37.287 --> 00:11:38.950
And I said, "You know what?

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:41.580
If I could have a
superpower, it would just be

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.280
to turn everything that
is bad for me to eat

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:45.450
into things that are good for me."

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.490
The whole thing would just flip.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.805
So it would be like, fried
chicken, processed meats,

00:11:50.805 --> 00:11:53.040
and not only would it tastes good,

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.730
but it would make me feel the way

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.370
that good, healthy food would feel

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:01.370
and be synthesized by your body.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.720
So did you tell them this

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.760
and did that just incriminate
you in what you were...

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.810
the thoughts you were consumed by?

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:12.230
So I was like, "I'm gonna go early

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.497
so I can enjoy everyone's answer."

00:12:14.497 --> 00:12:15.690
Oh, I like that.

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.140
So people got up and
said a couple of things

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:19.640
that were kinda fun, kind of interesting.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:20.797
And I was like the third and I said,

00:12:20.797 --> 00:12:23.250
"Well, I've actually
been thinking about this

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:27.370
'cause Jessie and I were
talking about this last week

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:30.610
and I said that I would turn
everything that's bad for me

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.310
into things that were good for me.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.964
And my first mission
would be to start eating.

